# {{.BinaryName}}

An epack collector built with the [Component SDK](https://github.com/locktivity/epack).

See [docs/](docs/) for setup and the output: [configuration](docs/configuration.md), [overview](docs/overview.md), [levels](docs/levels.md), and [examples](docs/examples.md).

## Layout

| Path | Contents |
|------|----------|
| `cmd/{{.BinaryName}}/main.go` | The entrypoint, which runs the handler |
| `internal/collector/handler.go` | The collector's spec and handler |
| `internal/collector/collector.go` | The collection, which builds the artifact |
| `internal/collector/gaps.go` | Records each value the collector leaves out, and why |
| `internal/{{.VendorPackage}}/client.go` | The API client, with one named operation per call |
| `docs/schema/v1.0.0.json` | The artifact's JSON Schema, with the `x-epack` annotations the output checks read |
| `docs/schema/trace-map.json` | The trace map, which links each field to the calls and the code behind it |

## Development

```bash
# Build
go build -o {{.BinaryName}} {{.MainPackage}}

# Test capabilities
./{{.BinaryName}} --capabilities

# Run locally (with epack SDK)
epack sdk run ./{{.BinaryName}}

# Watch mode: auto-rebuild on file changes
epack sdk run --watch .
```

## Testing

```bash
go test -race ./...
```

`internal/collector/collectortest_test.go` runs the collector against canned API responses: an organization written by hand in `fixtures_test.go`, one with no users, and the sandbox's recording once there is one. It then makes each API call fail in turn, with errors such as a 403, a timeout, or a malformed body, and checks that each value the collector could not read is left out with a gap. It also checks the output against `docs/schema/v1.0.0.json` at every collection level, and checks that the token never reaches another host or any output. Add a scenario for each state of an organization the collector has to handle.

### Recording the sandbox

`internal/collector/record_test.go` runs the collector once against a sandbox, an organization kept with known settings, and writes the responses to `internal/collector/testdata/recordings/{{.Name}}.json`, which the suite loads as one more scenario. It reads the organization from `{{.SandboxEnv}}` and the token from `{{.TokenEnv}}`:

```bash
{{.SandboxEnv}}=your-org {{.TokenEnv}}=your-token make record
```

The recording names the organization by the kit's fake, and `recordScrubPatterns` in `recording_scrub_test.go` replace the names of the organization and its users. Record also replaces emails, public IP addresses, and the token. It writes nothing while the token or the value of a `SANDBOX_` variable remains, and `TestRecordingsScrubbed` checks the committed recording again. Add a pattern for each field in the vendor's responses that holds a person's details or names the organization.

### Trace map

`docs/schema/trace-map.json` links each field of the artifact to the calls whose failure leaves it out or changes it, the source lines behind it, and the lines that record its gaps. The collector embeds it, and every pack carries it in the collector's build file. After a change to the code, rewrite it and commit the result:

```bash
make trace-map
```

CI fails when the committed map is stale, and so does `go test` when the suite finds a call or field the map lacks.

### Conformance

```bash
make conformance
```

This runs the conformance harness at the epack version `go.mod` pins, with `testdata/conformance.json` as the config and a canary value in each variable `CONFORMANCE_SECRETS` lists in the Makefile. Keep the config free of secrets. Conformance fails if any output prints a canary, and checks that a rejected token exits 3. `go test` fails when `CONFORMANCE_SECRETS` or `conformance-secrets` in `.github/workflows/ci.yaml` leaves out a credential the kit signs in with.

## CI

`.github/workflows/ci.yaml` calls epack's shared collector workflow, which runs the tests, lint, release builds, conformance, and govulncheck. On a pull request it also compares each scenario's output with the output of the latest release.

## Release

Tag a version to trigger the release workflow:

```bash
git tag v0.1.0
git push origin v0.1.0
```

The workflow runs CI, builds binaries for linux and darwin on amd64 and arm64 with [SLSA Level 3](https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.0/levels#build-l3) provenance, and creates the GitHub release. When the repository variable `SANDBOX_RELEASE_CHECK` is `true`, the release starts as a draft, and `publish.yaml` publishes it once the sandbox check passes on the tag's commit. The repository variable `SANDBOX_STATUS_SENDER` names the account whose status counts.

